      "dom_id": "question-35169",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(2,-1)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$",
